- op: discuss  # 0bdc
  at: '2026-08-29T07:18:10Z'  # 0bdc
  by: agent  # 0bdc
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 0bdc
  clock: 4  # 0bdc
  nonce: 0bdc  # 0bdc
  message: "UNBLOCKED — WI-johuk reached a resolved state via ADR-0049 ('A Deferred Crossing Is Disclosed, Not Minted').\n\nWHAT THE RULING GIVES YOU. The discriminator is: does the call return, to its own caller, a value whose content is chosen by the far side? Go's syscall/unix {Socket,Bind,Listen} return a descriptor and nothing else, so they are deferred crossings under ADR-0049 ruling 1 -- the same verdict WI-dosov already reached for Haskell when it removed socket/bind/listen while keeping accept.\n\nTWO CORRECTIONS TO THIS ITEM'S SCOPE, BOTH FROM THE ADR WORK.\n\n1. ACCEPT IS NOT IN SCOPE. syscall.Accept, syscall.Accept4, unix.Accept, unix.Accept4 and net.Listener.Accept return a peer-chosen address or connection TO THEIR CALLER, so they are TRANSFERS and stay net_recv on their own merits (ADR-0049 ruling 4). They are now asserted as such across seven languages in TestServerLaunchStaysAReceive, explicitly so a family-wide retag cannot take them along. If this item's '8 setup rows' was counted to include the Accept variants, the count is wrong.\n\n2. PART OF THIS MAY ALREADY BE ADJUDICATED. Measurement 0006 §A carries the row 'c | socket | net_recv | returns a descriptor; receives nothing' -- an adjudicated verdict AGAINST a member of this family. Go's rows are the same shape in a different language.\n\nSEQUENCING. ADR-0049 ruling 3 licenses no row move before a represented crossing exists, so this item now runs behind WI-hazop (census) and WI-vapud (reachability per row) like the rest of the family. Run the reachability check first: flask.Flask.run turned out to be in python's ambiguous_names and mints nothing at all, and nobody has checked whether go's setup rows actually fire.\n\nTHE OTHER HALF OF THIS ITEM STANDS AND IS NOT ADDRESSED BY THE ADR: the F2 socket gate is blind to Go twice over -- a hardcoded 9-language scope AND a case-sensitive predicate against a language that capitalises every export. Adding Go to the list alone would still report clean. That is an instrument defect, not a catalogue defect, and it is the part of this item that no ruling can settle."  # 0bdc
- op: discuss  # d405
  at: '2026-08-29T08:16:08Z'  # d405
  by: agent  # d405
  actor: jgstern_agent  # d405
  clock: 5  # d405
  nonce: d405  # d405
  message: "ADJUDICATED. The gate half is FIXED; the row half is NOT LICENSED, and the measurement says exactly why.\n\nWHAT SHIPPED: THE GATE. test_socket_setup_is_not_a_network_receive was blind twice over and both halves are closed. (1) SCOPE — F2_LANGS was a hardcoded nine names against fifteen shipped catalogues; the parametrisation is now derived from the tree by globbing io_primitives/*.yaml, so a catalogue added tomorrow is asked the F2 question on the commit that adds it. Deliberately NOT CATALOG_LANGUAGES, which names fourteen and would have reproduced the same decay. (2) CASE — the predicate is case-folded. RED PROOF: with the go exemption lifted, the widened gate fails naming all eight rows (unix.Socket/Bind/Listen, syscall.Socket/Bind/Listen, net.Listen, fiber.App.Listen). It catches what it was blind to.\n\nTwo new instrument gates ride along. test_the_f2_scope_is_derived_from_the_tree_not_maintained_beside_it asserts the derivation equals the tree and that F2_LANGS is a strict subset. test_the_f2_predicate_is_case_insensitive pins the fold on the MATCHER, independently of whether any language currently has an offender.\n\nWHAT DID NOT SHIP, AND WHY: THE ROWS. This item's filed licence — 'net.Conn.Read and syscall.Recvfrom carry the actual receive, so the F2/F3 licence rule is satisfiable here' — is TRUE OF THE CATALOGUE AND FALSE OF THE RUN. I wrote that claim myself in the unblock note above by reading rows rather than running them, and it is wrong.\n\nMEASURED on an idiomatic Go accept loop (net.Listen -> ln.Accept -> conn.Read -> os.WriteFile; rig ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/kanuk_go_08292026):\n\n  net_recv: 4 chains\n    syscall.Socket    src=rawSetup\n    syscall.Bind      src=rawSetup\n    syscall.Listen    src=rawSetup\n    net.Listen        src=serve\n\nEvery reported receive is a setup call that receives nothing. ln.Accept() and conn.Read() emit dst go:external:0-0:Accept / :Read — the module slot is 'external', so no net.Listener.Accept or net.Conn.Read row can ever match. external_potential_edges is 0, so they are not even disclosed as unresolved; they are absent.\n\nSo removing the four firing rows takes go's network-input surface to ZERO rather than relocating it. ADR-0049 ruling 3 licenses a removal only while the crossing stays represented, and here it would not be. That is the WI-lunav failure at four rows instead of one, and it is exactly what ruling 3's 'run, not reasoned' clause exists to catch.\n\nTHE BLOCKER IS FILED AS WI-doluf (P1, parent INV-linub), now isbefore this item. A DECLARED external receiver type reaches the module slot (func f(conn net.Conn) -> go:net:0-0:Read, matches); one INFERRED FROM A RETURN VALUE does not. The positive control is what makes that attributable: makeConn() is declared IN-REPO returning net.Conn, so the INV-dihos registry has something to register, and conn.Read() STILL emits go:external:0-0:Read. Without that control I would have filed 'stdlib has no declaration to register', which is the wrong mechanism.\n\nSCOPE CORRECTION TO THIS ITEM. Its description says the 8 rows are setup calls. Under ADR-0049 ruling 4 they are two shapes: six are setup (unix/syscall Socket/Bind/Listen) and net.Listen is arguably setup too, but github.com/gofiber/fiber/v2.App.Listen is a BLOCKING SERVE LOOP — a launch, not setup. When the removal is eventually licensed, fiber needs its own answer, because no sibling row represents a fiber app's ingress at all.\n\nSTATUS STAYS VIOLATED. The statement — 'no connection-SETUP call is declared net_recv in any catalogued language' — is still false in go, and nothing in this change made it true. What changed is that the violation is now VISIBLE to the gate instead of invisible to it, and the thing preventing the fix is named and filed rather than assumed absent."  # d405
